A||N||30||grgoyl86||Dream Garage Ideas||||6||
Z||000000||grgoyl86||07-22-2001||09:32 PM||||Hi Folks!<P>I am nearing the completion of the shell of my new garage. It's a four-car garage with a full-length attic above. If you want to see it being built, go to <A HREF="http://www.onparadiseroad.com" TARGET=_blank>www.onparadiseroad.com</A>  and click on the New Garage link.<P>Anyway, I am planning the inside, and was looking for tips on what "must-haves" people really like. What did you put in your garage that you REALLY like, or that was a mistake. What do you wish you would have put in there? What have you seen that you thought was really cool?<P>Keep in mind that we have spent a lot getting it this far, so the budget is REALLY low now. 8-)<P>Thanks for your suggestions!<P>Bob||||reg||6
Z||000001||LT1 Chop Top||07-22-2001||09:37 PM||||Definitely air conditioning, a groove deep enough to comfortably slide a creeper under a car to change the oil, and an epoxy floor so the tools bounce!!||||reg||12
Z||000002||kempo||07-22-2001||09:40 PM||||You need a fridge and a sink!  Definitely a fridge!  And stuff to put in it (ahem...)<P>-Kemp<BR>'87 Black GT / 5 Speed||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||07-22-2001||09:46 PM||||You can't live without a lift and air compressor. I know the lift is costly, about $2500 but a great investment. If I ever get a garage. those would top my list.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Uaana||07-22-2001||09:50 PM||||If they haven't poured the foundation yet.. <BR>deff get drains installed! <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000005||AusFiero||07-22-2001||09:50 PM||||I used to have a 4 car garage once with a pit that you could just walk under the car. Man it was awesome. Shame it was a rental.<BR>Higly recommended to new garage builders who want to make life easy for working on cars.<BR>Definately climate control it. Makes those late night work sessions so much more comfortable.<P>Oh did I mention the bar fridge. Also a must.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000006||Terrybogin||07-22-2001||09:52 PM||||My vote also goes to a good lift. I bought a used drive on from an old tire store that was being torn down because of a highway expansion, it was priced "right"! Never again will I crawl under a car to change oil, oh life is good. . .||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||07-23-2001||01:00 AM||||Aus, they outlawed pits here as they are firetraps.  I would definitely put a lift in or prep for one in the near future.  Also, seeing how my garage hits 95F during the day, I would also put in AC.  How tall is the ceiling?  I have a 3 car garage and 2 still sit outside...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||07-23-2001||03:18 AM||||Pour the pit, then cap it. Tear the cap off after inspection  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Dream garage:<P>-lift<BR>-chainfall on a dolly, with a nice long L-shaped track<BR>-floor to ceiling shelving<BR>-full-length flourescent banks<BR>-heat, air.<BR>-Exhaust evac system/air recirculator<BR>-Fridge<BR>-Fully plumbed sink w/ small electric gas heater<BR>-unplumbed SS sink for part washing. Drains into storage drum/solvent reservoir-pump<BR>-Sturdy built in work benches with SS tops<BR>-Rails in ceiling to accomodate vinyl curtains for spray painting.<BR>-FULLY FINISHED WALLS to reduce dust<BR>-Built in 10+ HP compressor, w/ fittings plumbed around walls<BR>-built in audio system<BR>-Built in Alarm system (intruder, fire, CO)<BR>-MINIMUM 100amp service panel with own main breakers.<P>That's about what I'D do if money was no object  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierobaby||07-23-2001||12:03 PM||||geee we havn't given much thought to the perfect gagrage...mine would just be a HUGE garage full of Fieros and all kinds of other fun goodys ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierobear||07-23-2001||12:09 PM||||I told my wife that our next house needs to be a very large garage with a house attached. <BR>She can keep the house. If you need me, I'll be in my GARAGE (echo, echo, echo).||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||07-23-2001||12:11 PM||||I want a house to live in with a 6 car underground garage beneath it. Cool, burgler proof. <P>Make sure you make plenty of storage just for loose crap.||||reg||1
Z||000012||AusFiero||07-23-2001||12:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I want a house to live in with a 6 car underground garage beneath it. Cool, burgler proof. <P>Make sure you make plenty of storage just for loose crap.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In my architectural design days Roger I designed my dream house along those lines. The 3 car garage had a car elevator that could lower your car down to a workshop as large as the house itself. We don't have uses for basements in Australia but god that would be a good use for one.<P>Mind you the house was a big design. Coulda parked about 40 cars down there.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||07-23-2001||04:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I want a house to live in with a 6 car underground garage beneath it. Cool, burgler proof.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Holy cow Roger, we think alike.  I've even got some basic drawings that an architect friend of mine drew.  Wish land prices were cheaper here in Pittsburgh, of course the Ohio border (and cheaper land) is only about 35 miles away.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||artherd||07-23-2001||06:09 PM||||Essentials: <BR>Epoxy floor!<BR>Insulated, finished walls, HVAC total climate control.<P>Lift. (you can plan for one later)<P>Also, chain hoist in the celing (on rails) make SURE you engineer it to hold the weight!<P>full size fridge :) bar, etc. :)<P>:)<P>My dream garage is in Ferris Buler's Day Off.<BR>Ferrari included.<P>ENJOY!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000015||StuGood||07-23-2001||06:22 PM||||Not very original, nor "dreamy," but here are a few of the simple things I have in my home garage, that turned out to be winners:<P>Two single doors rather than one double door (others will differ with this, I'm sure), with a 2-foot-wide piece of wall between ("septum"?).  Lets in less cold air while opening/closing in winter.  At least <B>one</B> garage door+ opener always works.<P>A 2-foot-wide "peninsula" workbench runs from that "septum," parallel and in between two cars parked on each side. Advantages -if need bench for big stuff that overhangs the bench, can put cars out on driveway and get at workbench from both sides.  Sure beats dragging big stuff to bench in back wall of garage, only to have it hit the wall.<P>Hung (vertical tank) air compressor from the rafters, in center of garage.  Frees up much real estate in garage.  Drain valve (on bottom of tank) is just above eye level - much more convenient to reach up and drain tank, than squat down to base of floor-mounted tank.  On bottom of tank, plumbed a short length of nipple, teed in a pressure gage, then a ball valve - much easier to use than OE petcock.  The motor+ compressor reside in the attic, so less noise.  Hoses are coiled+hung on compressor tank's "feet."<BR>Of course, have to climb ladder to check oil level, but you don't do that often as drain tank. <BR>||||reg||3
Z||000016||rogergarrison||07-23-2001||08:11 PM||||Id rather the air compressor in the attic. a little room all to itself with lots of soundproofing. I hate trying to hear races on my tv while im working. my shop has a built in 20 inch color with cable and VCR. those are must haves for me, lots better than music all day long.(&lt;-opinion)<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||DXR_DAD||07-23-2001||09:44 PM||||The last thing I added to the shop was the sand blast cabnet, let me say...It should have been the first!!! It's a must.<P>bench Grinder/buffer, mig welder, drill press, parts washer. Just a few of the little thing that make life a little easy<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000018||Dragon||07-23-2001||10:07 PM||||I like having a removable eye hook in the floor and a electric wench for dragging in dead Fieros.<P>The same electric wench could be used for lifting said broken Fiero with a A-Frame set up.  Works great for swaping engines.  And gives you flexability.<P>------------------<BR>Dragon<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spacecoastfieros.com/dragon.jpg">  <P>   Space Coast Fieros||||reg||14
Z||000019||operator64||07-23-2001||11:00 PM||||   The most important thing besides the refreshment fridge is of course the Bathroom, the floors in the house will thank you for it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||87GTBro||07-23-2001||11:30 PM||||On the underground garage idea... Have a ramp leading down under the house with a sliding "trap" door made out of steel I-beams and covered with corrugated stainless steel sheet metal.  Put a thin layer of dirt and sod on top of that to disguise the door.<P>The door is operated by a heavy-duty garage door opener which pulls the door underneath the house on tracks with ball bearings.  Then closes again hiding the passageway.  (i.e.)  It looks like a patch of grass.<P>Nobody will ever know you have a garage!||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||07-24-2001||11:45 AM||||that color looks good on your car Dragon. and like I said before, im not big on green. You have tan or gray interior?||||reg||1
Z||000022||deceler8||07-24-2001||01:05 PM||||South facing skylights...for natural light and warmth in the wintertime.||||reg||1
Z||000023||grgoyl86||07-24-2001||03:14 PM||||Thanks for all the advice! Some great stuff in there!<BR>Some of these ideas are in the plans, but will have to wait for more funds. Really blew the budget just to get it built.<BR>I really liked dragon's idea of the eyebolts in the floor. I am getting the concrete floor done this Saturday, so I jumped right on that one. I emailed a structural engineer I used to work with to ask him about the size of the bolts, how to get them in there etc. His first comment was "I thought Fieros never break down". Anyway, he gave me some good suggestions, and I will jump on them.<P>Keep the ideas coming!||||reg||14
Z||000024||Dumba$$||07-25-2001||01:29 AM||||hoist,toilet,fridge,bigscreen,leather couch    (tv & couch are for when you get<BR>really madd when working on your car and need a rest<P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE||||reg||1
